xnu 0251: time a submission, and how much of it is the buffer writeback

The frame line's "handing on" is glFlush(), which is Mesa's work plus one call
into this driver, and it ranged from 0.4 ms to 921 ms across the frames of one
boot with no draw count to explain it: a 202-call frame cost 17.8 ms while a
1159-call one cost 22.5 ms, so the cost is per submission, not per call. What
this side of the boundary can measure is which part of a submission it is, and
the writeback of the buffers the CPU may have written is the largest candidate
(a frame submits the 23 MB display surface, 4500 pages of getPhysicalSegment
and flush_dcache64).

gpusubtime=1 logs one line a submission: the time the whole client call took,
the slice the CPU-side writeback (bufferSync) took, how many client calls
arrived since the submission before, how many buffers were written back and
how many bytes, the commands and the fence. Next to the frame's own line it
says whether a slow frame is waiting on the writeback, on the ring, or on
Mesa, without a bisect. Off unless the boot argument asks for it.
--- a/iokit/Kernel/arm/MondrianGPU.cpp
+++ b/iokit/Kernel/arm/MondrianGPU.cpp
@@ -245,8 +245,30 @@
 	bool f2dFaultDone;          /* the unmapped-destination blit ran */
 	bool f2dBigDone;            /* the oversized blit ran */
 	uint32_t fSyncLogged;
+	uint32_t fEntries;          /* gpusubtime=1: client calls since the last submission */
+	uint32_t fSubmitLogged;
 };
 
+/*
+ * gpusubtime=1: one line a submission with the time the whole call took, the
+ * slice of it the CPU-side writeback (bufferSync) took, how many client calls
+ * arrived since the submission before, and how much memory was written back.
+ * The frame line's "handing on" is glFlush(), which is this function plus
+ * Mesa's own work in the process; this says which of the two is the frame.
+ */
+static bool
+mondrian_sub_time(void)
+{
+	static int parsed;
+	static int on;
+
+	if (parsed == 0) {
+		parsed = 1;
+		(void)PE_parse_boot_argn("gpusubtime", &on, sizeof(on));
+	}
+	return on != 0;
+}
+
 class MondrianGPU : public IOService
 {
 	OSDeclareDefaultStructors(MondrianGPU);
@@ -351,6 +373,8 @@
